  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Queues are one of the most fundamental data structures in computer science and one of the most practical ones too.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You will find them powering real systems all around you:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246257694-158702168.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "span-style-font-size-2-5rem-letter-spacing-0-003em-1-what-is-a-queue-span-br",
      "title": "<span style=\"font-size: 2.5rem; letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">1. What is a Queue?</span><br>",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A queue is a linear data structure that processes elements in&nbsp;<strong>exactly the order they arrived</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246263603-947522005.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">The rule it follows is called&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">FIFO First In, First Out</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246265604-9063225.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></span>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>FIFO Order (First In, First Out):</strong>The first element inserted at the&nbsp;<strong>front&nbsp;</strong>is the first one to be removed.<strong>Operations:&nbsp;</strong><strong>Enqueue&nbsp;</strong>adds elements at the&nbsp;<strong>back</strong>, and&nbsp;<strong>Dequeue&nbsp;</strong>removes elements from the&nbsp;<strong>front</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Order matters (FIFO):</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Just like your example,&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Raj (first)</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">is served first, and&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Kiran (last)</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">waits the longest.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Queue flow:</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">New people (</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">enqueue</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">) join at the&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">back</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, and serving (</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">dequeue</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">) always happens from the&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">front</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Nobody</b> jumps the line. <b>Nobody</b> gets served from the middle. Strictly in order.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-core-queue-operations",
      "title": "2. Core Queue Operations",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Let's walk through each operation one by one with a fresh example.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "1. Enqueue",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Adds a new element to the&nbsp;<strong>back</strong>&nbsp;of the queue.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Starting with an empty queue, we enqueue three values:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the first step, when&nbsp;<strong>Enqueue(5)&nbsp;</strong>is performed, the queue is empty, so the element&nbsp;<strong>5&nbsp;</strong>is added and both the&nbsp;<strong>front&nbsp;</strong>and&nbsp;<strong>back&nbsp;</strong>point to it.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246267231-227178072.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246267231-227178072.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246267231-227178072.png",
          "alt": "Enqueue(5) on an empty queue"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the second step, when&nbsp;<strong>Enqueue(15)&nbsp;</strong>is performed, the element<strong>15</strong>is added at the&nbsp;<strong>back&nbsp;</strong>of the queue, while the&nbsp;<strong>front&nbsp;</strong>remains at<strong>5</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246268799-554142321.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the third step, when&nbsp;<strong>Enqueue(25)&nbsp;</strong>is performed, the element&nbsp;<strong>25&nbsp;</strong>is added at the&nbsp;<strong>back</strong>, and now the queue becomes&nbsp;<strong>5 → 15 → 25</strong>, maintaining the FIFO order.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246270348-732092148.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Time: O(1)</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "2. Dequeue",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Removes the element at the&nbsp;<strong>front</strong>&nbsp;of the queue.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the first step, the queue contains&nbsp;<strong>5 → 15 → 25</strong>, where<strong>5</strong>is at the&nbsp;<strong>front&nbsp;</strong>and will be removed first.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When&nbsp;<strong>Dequeue()&nbsp;</strong>is performed, the element&nbsp;<strong>5&nbsp;</strong>is removed from the&nbsp;<strong>front&nbsp;</strong>of the queue.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246271972-638788412.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "After removal, the&nbsp;<strong>front moves to 15</strong>, and the queue becomes&nbsp;<strong>15 → 25</strong>, while the&nbsp;<strong>back remains at 25</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246273465-242463130.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Time: O(1)</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "3. Peek",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Lets you&nbsp;<strong>look</strong>&nbsp;at the front element without removing it.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246274999-71664162.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246274999-71664162.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246274999-71664162.png",
          "alt": "Peek() looks at the front element without removing it"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Peek operation&nbsp;</strong>allows you to view the element at the&nbsp;<strong>front&nbsp;</strong>of the queue without removing it.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246276482-491660037.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In this example, the front element is&nbsp;<strong>15</strong>, so&nbsp;<strong>Peek() returns 15</strong>.The queue remains unchanged (<strong>15 → 25</strong>) since no element is removed.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Time: O(1)</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "4. isEmpty",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Checks whether the queue has any elements left.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the first case, the queue contains elements (<strong>15 → 25</strong>), so&nbsp;<strong>isEmpty() returns false&nbsp;</strong>because the queue is not empty.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The function&nbsp;<strong>is Empty() checks whether the queue has any elements&nbsp;</strong>by verifying if the front is null or not.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246277984-153712068.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the second case, when the queue has no elements,&nbsp;<strong>isEmpty() returns true</strong>, indicating the queue is empty.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785246280420-601033779.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Time: O(1)</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "All four operations run in constant time that is what makes queues so efficient for real-world systems.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-queue-implementations",
      "title": "3. Queue Implementations",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "There are multiple ways to actually build a queue. Let's go through the most common ones.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "1. Using an Array",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The most straightforward approach. You keep two pointers one tracking the front, one tracking the back.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246283305-491448136.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246283305-491448136.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246283305-491448136.png",
          "alt": "Array-based queue with front and back pointers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>The problem:&nbsp;</strong>every time you dequeue from the front, those slots become permanently wasted:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>After 2&nbsp;<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Dequeue</span></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246285161-73200460.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246285161-73200460.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246285161-73200460.png",
          "alt": "Wasted array slots after two dequeue operations"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "After performing two&nbsp;<strong>dequeue operations</strong>, the front moves forward, leaving the earlier positions (index 0 and 1) unused.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These empty positions cannot be reused in a simple array-based queue, so they become&nbsp;<strong>wasted space</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Even though there is space available, new elements cannot be added there, which leads to&nbsp;<strong>inefficient memory usage</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "The fix:&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "1. Circular Array",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When the rear pointer hits the end, it wraps back around to slot 0 if space is free. This reuses the freed-up slots from earlier dequeues.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246287440-496080407.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246287440-496080407.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246287440-496080407.png",
          "alt": "Circular array queue wrapping the rear pointer to slot 0"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In a&nbsp;<strong>circular array queue</strong>, when the&nbsp;<strong>rear reaches the end</strong>, it wraps back to index&nbsp;<strong>0&nbsp;</strong>using<code>rear = (rear + 1) % capacity</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This allows the queue to&nbsp;<strong>reuse the empty slots&nbsp;</strong>created by earlier dequeue operations instead of wasting space.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "As a result, memory is used efficiently, and the queue can continue inserting elements without needing extra space.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "class ArrayQueue {\n    int[] arr;\n    int front, rear, size, capacity;\n\n    public ArrayQueue(int capacity) {\n        this.capacity = capacity;\n        arr = new int[capacity];\n        front = 0;\n        rear = -1;\n        size = 0;\n    }\n\n    public void enqueue(int x) {\n        if (size == capacity) throw new RuntimeException(\"Queue full\");\n        rear = (rear + 1) % capacity;   // wrap around\n        arr[rear] = x;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public int dequeue() {\n        if (size == 0) throw new RuntimeException(\"Queue empty\");\n        int val = arr[front];\n        front = (front + 1) % capacity; // wrap around\n        size--;\n        return val;\n    }\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Both&nbsp;<strong>front&nbsp;</strong>and&nbsp;<strong>rear&nbsp;</strong>use modulo (<code>% capacity</code>) to wrap around, so the queue stays&nbsp;<strong>compact&nbsp;</strong>without shifting elements.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This circular behavior ensures&nbsp;<strong>no space is wasted</strong>, as freed slots are reused efficiently.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Both operations are efficient:&nbsp;<strong>Enqueue = O(1)</strong>and<strong>Dequeue = O(1)</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "2. Using a LinkedList",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246289244-543452371.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246289244-543452371.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246289244-543452371.png",
          "alt": "Linked list queue with head/front and tail/rear pointers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A&nbsp;<strong>linked list queue&nbsp;</strong>grows and shrinks dynamically, so there is&nbsp;<strong>no fixed size&nbsp;</strong>and no wasted space.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It does not require circular logic or shifting, since nodes are created and removed as needed.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Two pointers are maintained:&nbsp;<strong>head (front, for dequeue)</strong>and&nbsp;<strong>tail (back, for enqueue)</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "class ListQueue {\n    class Node {\n        int val;\n        Node next;\n        Node(int val) { this.val = val; }\n    }\n\n    Node head, tail;\n\n    public void enqueue(int x) {\n        Node node = new Node(x);\n        if (tail != null) tail.next = node;\n        tail = node;\n        if (head == null) head = node;   // first element\n    }\n\n    public int dequeue() {\n        if (head == null) throw new RuntimeException(\"Queue empty\");\n        int val = head.val;\n        head = head.next;\n        if (head == null) tail = null;   // queue is now empty\n        return val;\n    }\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For enqueue new node attaches to tail, tail moves forward. For dequeue read head's value, move head forward. If head becomes null, tail is reset too.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Enqueue: O(1) &nbsp;Dequeue: O(1)</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "3. Using Built-in Libraries",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In interviews and real projects you almost never build a queue from scratch. Every major language ships one:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();\n\nq.offer(5);      // enqueue\nq.offer(15);\nq.offer(25);\n\nq.poll();        // dequeue → returns 5\nq.peek();        // front   → returns 15\nq.isEmpty();     // false"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Unless the interviewer asks you to build one from scratch, use the built-in it is optimized and tested.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-types-of-queues",
      "title": "4. Types of Queues",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The basic queue is just the starting point. There are four main variations, each designed for specific use cases.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "1. Simple Queue",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The standard version you’ve already seen. Elements are enqueued at the back and dequeued from the front strictly FIFO. Nothing more.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246291076-443220677.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246291076-443220677.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246291076-443220677.png",
          "alt": "Simple queue with front and back pointers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When implemented using a plain array, it wastes space over time which is why the circular queue was introduced.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "2. Circular Queue",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Same concept as a simple queue, but the rear wraps around to reuse freed slots at the front.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246294080-446989361.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246294080-446989361.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246294080-446989361.png",
          "alt": "Circular queue reusing freed slots via wraparound"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is especially useful in memory-constrained systems like audio/video buffers and network packet handling.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "3. Deque (Double-Ended Queue)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A deque allows insertion and removal from&nbsp;<strong>both ends&nbsp;</strong>front and back.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246295977-959824677.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246295977-959824677.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246295977-959824677.png",
          "alt": "Deque allowing insertion and removal from both ends"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If you want, I can also refine this into your<strong>DSA course JSON format</strong>or add real-world examples for each type.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();\n\ndq.addFirst(10);    // add to front\ndq.addLast(40);     // add to back\ndq.pollFirst();     // remove from front\ndq.pollLast();      // remove from back"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Much more flexible than a regular queue. Sliding window problems in interviews almost always use a deque.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "4. Priority Queue",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A priority queue does&nbsp;<strong>not</strong>&nbsp;follow FIFO. Each element is associated with a priority, and the element with the highest priority is removed first regardless of when it was inserted.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246297509-931045701.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246297509-931045701.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785246297509-931045701.png",
          "alt": "Priority queue exit order compared to a regular FIFO queue"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In a&nbsp;<strong>regular queue</strong>, elements are processed in&nbsp;<strong>FIFO order</strong>, so<strong>30 exits first&nbsp;</strong>because it arrived first.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "In a&nbsp;<strong>priority queue</strong>, elements are processed based on&nbsp;<strong>priority (value)</strong>, so&nbsp;<strong>10 exits first&nbsp;</strong>because it has the highest priority (smallest value).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Unlike a regular queue, a priority queue&nbsp;<strong>does not follow insertion order</strong>, but instead follows&nbsp;<strong>sorting/priority rules</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();\npq.add(30);\npq.add(10);\npq.add(50);\n\npq.poll();   // returns 10 — smallest exits first"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p><strong>Real-world use:</strong>Hospital emergency rooms. A critically ill patient is treated before someone with a minor injury regardless of who arrived first.</p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
